1.1 - OSI Model
Comptia objective: Explain concepts related to the Open Systems Interconnection (OSI) reference model.

OSI Model
7 - Application - Generates data for transmit. Processes data recieved
6 - Presentation - Data representation and encryption
5 - Session - Interhost communication
4 - Transport - End to end connections and reliability (TCP/UDP)
3 - Network - Communication between DIFFERENT networks via IP (routing)
2 - Data link - Communication between SAME network via MAC addresses (switching)
1 - Physical - Media, signal, and binary transmissions (cables/wireless)
Anagram - All People Seem To Need Data Processing

7 - Application
Serves as the interface between the user and network services. Facilitates the end-user processes and apps to access network services
Defines protocols for various network services like file transfer, email, and web browsing. Ensuring seamless communications between software apps and the network
Data is represented as DATA

6 - Presentation
Responsible for the translation, encryption, and compression of data between the app and network formats. 
Ensures that data is presented in a usable format and mediates between the data formats and protocols used by the network and apps
Data is represented as DATA

5 - Session (SSH, RDP...)
Establishes, manages and terminates connections between local and remote apps.
Provides mechanisms for controlling the dialog between the two end systems, either half-duplex or full-duplex
Common protocols include SSH and RDP

4 - Transport (TCP/UDP)
Responsible for providing reliable, trasparent transfer of data between end systems. Ensures complete data transfer with mechanisms for error correcting, flow control, and segmentation/de-segmentation of data
Enables seamless communication between devices by managaing end to end message delivery
TCP and UDP. Data is represented as SEGMENTS or DATAGRAMS

3 - Network (ROUTING/IP)
Responsible for the logical addressing and routing of packets across DIFFERENT networks.
Determines best path for data transmission from the source to destination using routing protocols.
Manages packet forwarding, including routing through intermediate routers, and handles network congestion and packet filtering
Data is represented as PACKETS

2 - Data Link (SWITCHING/MAC)
Responsible for node to node data transfer and error detection and correction in the physical layer
Establishes, maintains, and terminates connections between two physically connected devices
Also handles the framing of data packets, including addressing and is divided into two sublayers:
  -MAC (Media Access Control) layer - manages protocol access to the physical network medium
    Responsible for the addressing and channel access control mechanisms that enable several terminals or network nodes to communicate within a multipoint network, typicallying using MAC addresses
  -LLC (Logical Link Control) layer - provides multiplexing mechanisms that allow multple protocols (e.g. IP, IPX) to coexist within a multiaccess network and provides flow and error control
    Acts as an interface between the neworking software in the upper layers and the device hardware in the lower layers, ensuring data integrity and specifying which mechanisms are to be used for addressing and controlling the data link
Data is represented as FRAMES

1 - Physical (CABLING/HARDWARE)
Responsible for the physical transmission of data over network media
Deals with the hardware aspects of networking, including cabling, switches, and electrical signals/light pulses that carry data
This layer defines the standards for devices and media to connect and transmit raw bits rather than logical data packets
Data is represented as BITS
